      "text": "We can use the given angles and side lengths to determine which segment is the longest.\n\nFirst, let's consider the segments AC and BC. Since angle CBA is equal to angle BAD, we can deduce that triangle BAD is an isosceles triangle. This means that segment AB is equal in length to segment BC. This is also true of AC and BC, since angle ACB is equal to angle BDC.\n\nIn the above figure, segment AB is equal to segments BD and CD. Moreover, we know that side AD is equal to side CD. Hence, we obtain triangle ACD = triangle BCD. This means that the triangle ADC is isosceles as well, making segment AC equal in length to segment CD.\n\nAlso, we know that segment DE is equal to segment CE. Because angle BCE is equal to angle BED, we can deduce that segment CE is equal in length to segment BE.\n\nNote, by using the converse of AAS, we conclude that segment CE is congruent to segment EA. Hence, we can deduce that segment CD is congruent to segment CE.\n\nAlso, we know by the converse of ASA, that segment EA is congruent to segment EC. From this statement, it is evident that segment CE is congruent to segment EC. The same conclusion can be drawn about segment CD being congruent to segment DC.\n\nBecause segment AC is equal to segments CD and CE, we can deduce that segment AC is equal in length to the sum of segments CD and CE, or AC = CD + CE.\n\nBased on the above steps, we can conclude that segment AC is the longest.\n\nThe final value of segment AC is $9$, resulting in the answer $\\boxed{AC}$.",
